    "Sure! Let's imagine you have a special recipe for making bread that doesn't use the usual yeast that makes bread rise. Instead, you use a special mixture of flour and water that has some wild yeast and bacteria in it. These wild yeast and bacteria are like tiny living things that help make the bread rise and give it a tangy taste.\n\nThe yeast makes the bread rise by making lots of tiny bubbles inside the dough. These bubbles are like little balloons that get bigger and bigger, making the bread bigger and puffier. The bacteria make the bread taste a bit sour because they make a special acid that gives the bread its tangy",
